tests/test_charts.py
checkpoints/cp13_2.allowlist

# `out/model_report.json` is gitignored by `out/*` and needs `git add -f`.
# `.gitignore` is not on this allowlist, so it gets no negation of its own this
# checkpoint — the same treatment `out/razorpay_demo.json` had between CP12 and
# CP12.1. One line in `.gitignore` closes it whenever that file is next open.
#
# It is generated by a script run against committed code — `report.timing_block`
# for the ranks and spread, `_run` for the SF-2 decomposition — with no repo
# module modified, because none is on this allowlist. F21 originally wanted
# these written at training time by `train.py`; that remains the better home and
# needs `settle/agent/train.py` open to do it.

# --- Two numbers in the prescribed text did not survive checking ------------
#
# Both were corrected before being written, and both are recorded here because
# the checkpoint they belong to is the one about not carrying figures forward
# without re-deriving them.
#
# 1. The feature-to-rank pairing was transposed. The prescribed sentence read
#    "days_to_month_start, in_liquidity_window and day_of_month_at_dispatch rank
#    22, 34 and 40", which pairs positionally to days_to_month_start = 22.
#    Measured, it is day_of_month_at_dispatch 22, in_liquidity_window 34,
#    days_to_month_start 40 — the first and last are swapped. The names are
#    reordered to match; no rank changed.
#
# 2. `world.liquidity_window_days` moves the headline 0.30 points across the
#    sweep, not 0.6. Margins run 2.40, 2.40, 2.25, 2.15, 2.10 at 0.25x-4x, so
#    the span is 0.30. 0.6 does not correspond to any pair of those points.
#    Written as 0.30 everywhere.
#
# The conclusion is unaffected in both cases and is arguably stronger for the
# second: liquidity moves the headline half as much as the prescribed text said.

# --- One prescribed explanation was wrong, and the measurement replaced it ---
#
# F23 read: "B3's 35 is lower than B2's 86 for the same reason inverted — it
# exhausts contact budgets earlier." B3 makes 24,780 contacts against B2's
# 14,027, so it does not run out of contacts, and B3 is in OBSERVE where the
# gates that would impose a budget do not bind.
#
# Measured instead. SF-2 needs a settlement the agent never heard about AND a
# contact after it, so the blind set is the denominator:
#
#   arm   settled   blind set   SF-2   share of blind set
#   B2      4,943         984     86               8.7%
#   B3      6,494         384     35               9.1%
#   OURS    5,122         976      1               0.1%
#
# B3 acts so much more that more of its settlements get reported at all, so its
# blind set is a third of B2's. Per blind case it is WORSE than B2. It has fewer
# opportunities to make the mistake, not more discipline — which is the same
# shape as the prescribed claim and a different mechanism, and the mechanism is
# the part a reader would check.
#
# OURS has the largest blind set of any acting arm and converts 0.1% of it. That
# is the number the restraint claim actually rests on, and it is a better number
# than the bare count of 1.
